::ALG2_U02_L05_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L05_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Which features are enough to begin sketching f(x) = -2(x + 1)(x - 4)? {
=zeros and end behavior #Correct. Zeros anchor the graph on the x-axis, and end behavior shows where the arms go.
~axis of symmetry only #Higher-degree polynomials may not have one axis of symmetry.
~y-intercept only #One point is not enough to sketch the graph.
~constant term only #The constant term alone does not show zeros or end behavior.
}

::ALG2_U02_L05_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L05_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>For f(x) = (x - 3)<sup>2</sup>(x + 2), what happens at x = 3? {
~the graph crosses #Crossing is typical of odd multiplicity.
=the graph touches and turns #Correct. The zero x = 3 has even multiplicity 2.
~the graph has no x-intercept #x = 3 is a real zero.
~the graph has a vertical asymptote #Polynomial graphs do not have vertical asymptotes.
}

::ALG2_U02_L05_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L05_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>For f(x) = (x + 5)(x - 1)<sup>2</sup>, where does the graph usually cross the x-axis? {
~x = 1 #That zero has even multiplicity and usually touches.
~x = 5 #This reverses the sign.
=x = -5 #Correct. The factor x + 5 has multiplicity 1, so the graph usually crosses there.
~x = -1 #This reverses the sign.
}

::ALG2_U02_L05_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L05_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>What is the end behavior of f(x) = -x<sup>4</sup> + 6x<sup>2</sup>? {
~both ends rise #That would require a positive leading coefficient.
~left falls, right rises #That is positive odd-degree behavior.
~left rises, right falls #That is negative odd-degree behavior.
=both ends fall #Correct. The leading term has even degree and a negative coefficient.
}

::ALG2_U02_L05_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L05_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>What point does the y-intercept help place on a polynomial sketch? {
=where the graph crosses the y-axis #Correct. The y-intercept is f(0), the output when x = 0.
~where the graph crosses the x-axis #x-intercepts come from zeros.
~where both ends go #End behavior comes from degree and leading coefficient.
~where the graph has a vertical asymptote #Polynomial functions do not have vertical asymptotes.
}

::ALG2_U02_L05_Q06::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L05_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Which graph behavior matches a positive cubic with zeros -2, 1, and 4? {
~rises left and falls right #That is negative cubic behavior.
=falls left and rises right #Correct. A positive cubic has left end down and right end up.
~both ends rise #That is positive even-degree behavior.
~both ends fall #That is negative even-degree behavior.
}

::ALG2_U02_L05_Q07::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L05_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>For f(x) = x(x - 2)(x + 3), which point is the y-intercept? {
~(2, 0) #This is an x-intercept.
~(-3, 0) #This is an x-intercept.
=(0, 0) #Correct. Since one factor is x, f(0) = 0.
~(0, -6) #Substituting x = 0 gives 0.
}

::ALG2_U02_L05_Q08::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L05_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Which zero should be marked as a touch point for f(x) = (x + 4)<sup>2</sup>(x - 6)? {
~x = 6 #This zero has multiplicity 1 and usually crosses.
~x = 4 #This reverses the sign.
~x = -6 #This reverses the sign.
=x = -4 #Correct. The factor x + 4 is squared, so its zero has even multiplicity.
}

::ALG2_U02_L05_Q09::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L05_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>When sketching f(x) = 3(x - 1)(x + 2)(x - 5), what should happen at each zero? {
=the graph crosses at each zero #Correct. Each factor has multiplicity 1, which is odd.
~the graph touches at each zero #Touching would require even multiplicity.
~the graph avoids the x-axis #Real zeros are x-axis contacts.
~the graph has holes at each zero #Polynomial graphs are continuous.
}

::ALG2_U02_L05_Q10::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L05_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement helps sketch f(x) = (x - 2)<sup>2</sup>(x + 1)<sup>2</sup>? {
~both zeros are crossing points #Crossing is typical of odd multiplicity.
=both zeros are touch points #Correct. Both factors have even multiplicity.
~there are no x-intercepts #Both factors give real zeros.
~only x = -1 is a zero #x = 2 is also a zero.
}

::ALG2_U02_L05_Q11::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L05_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>For f(x) = x<sup>3</sup> - 4x, which zeros help sketch the graph? {
~x = -4 and 4 #Those come from the constant but are not zeros.
~x = 0 only #Factoring shows three zeros.
=x = -2, 0, and 2 #Correct. Factoring gives x(x - 2)(x + 2).
~x = -1, 0, and 1 #Only x = 0 from this list is correct.
}

::ALG2_U02_L05_Q12::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L05_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Which feature determines whether the far-right side rises or falls? {
~middle term #Lower-degree terms do not control far-right behavior.
~constant term #The constant shifts values but does not control the far end.
~number of terms #Term count does not determine end behavior.
=leading term #Correct. The leading term controls end behavior.
}

::ALG2_U02_L05_Q13::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L05_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>For f(x) = -2(x + 3)(x - 1)<sup>2</sup>, what is the right-end behavior? {
=falls right #Correct. The degree is 3 and the leading coefficient is negative.
~rises right #That would match a positive odd-degree leading term.
~stays flat #A cubic end does not stay flat.
~approaches 0 #This polynomial does not approach 0 on the right.
}

::ALG2_U02_L05_Q14::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L05_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>What should be plotted from f(0) when sketching a polynomial? {
~the leading coefficient #The leading coefficient comes from the highest-degree term.
=the y-intercept #Correct. f(0) gives the graph point where x = 0.
~all x-intercepts #Zeros come from solving f(x) = 0.
~the axis of symmetry #Not every polynomial has one axis.
}

::ALG2_U02_L05_Q15::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L05_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Which factor form indicates the graph touches at x = 7? {
~(x + 7) #This gives zero -7 and multiplicity 1.
~(x - 7) #This gives zero 7 but usually crosses.
=(x - 7)<sup>2</sup> #Correct. The zero is 7 and the even exponent indicates a touch point.
~(x + 7)<sup>3</sup> #This gives zero -7 and odd multiplicity.
}

::ALG2_U02_L05_Q16::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L05_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>For f(x) = 2(x - 4)(x + 1), what is the y-intercept? {
~(4, 0) #This is an x-intercept.
~(-1, 0) #This is an x-intercept.
~(0, 8) #The product at x = 0 is negative.
=(0, -8) #Correct. f(0) = 2(-4)(1) = -8.
}

::ALG2_U02_L05_Q17::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L05_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Which sketching step should come after finding zeros and multiplicities? {
=use end behavior to shape the arms #Correct. End behavior tells how the far left and far right should look.
~ignore the leading coefficient #The leading coefficient is needed for end behavior.
~draw a line through all zeros #Polynomial graphs are not generally straight lines.
~remove repeated zeros #Repeated zeros affect touch/cross behavior.
}

::ALG2_U02_L05_Q18::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L05_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>A polynomial has zeros -3 and 2, both with odd multiplicity, and positive leading coefficient. What should the graph do at both zeros? {
~touch and turn #That is typical of even multiplicity.
=cross the x-axis #Correct. Odd multiplicity zeros usually cross the x-axis.
~create vertical asymptotes #Polynomial graphs do not have vertical asymptotes.
~avoid the x-axis #Zeros are points where the graph meets the x-axis.
}

::ALG2_U02_L05_Q19::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L05_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>What graph behavior matches f(x) = (x + 2)<sup>4</sup>? {
~crosses at x = -2 #Crossing is typical of odd multiplicity.
~touches at x = 2 #The zero from x + 2 is -2.
=touches at x = -2 #Correct. The even multiplicity zero causes the graph to touch and turn.
~has no x-intercept #The factor gives a real zero.
}

::ALG2_U02_L05_Q20::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L05_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Which feature is missing if you know only the zeros of a polynomial? {
~x-intercepts #Zeros give x-intercepts.
~root values #Zeros and roots are related values.
~where f(x) = 0 #Zeros are where f(x) = 0.
=end behavior #Correct. Zeros do not tell whether the graph rises or falls on the ends.
}

::ALG2_U02_L05_Q21::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L05_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>For f(x) = -x(x - 5)<sup>2</sup>, what happens at x = 5? {
=the graph touches and turns #Correct. The zero x = 5 has even multiplicity 2.
~the graph crosses #Crossing is typical of odd multiplicity.
~the graph is undefined #Polynomial functions are defined at x = 5.
~the graph has no contact with the x-axis #x = 5 is a zero.
}

::ALG2_U02_L05_Q22::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L05_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Which polynomial would have both ends rising and touch at x = 1? {
~-(x - 1)<sup>2</sup> #This falls on both ends.
=(x - 1)<sup>2</sup>(x + 3)<sup>2</sup> #Correct. This has even degree, positive leading coefficient, and even multiplicity at x = 1.
~(x - 1)(x + 3) #This has odd multiplicity at x = 1.
~-(x - 1)(x + 3)<sup>2</sup> #This has odd degree and negative leading behavior.
}

::ALG2_U02_L05_Q23::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L05_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement is most useful when sketching f(x) = x<sup>4</sup> - 16? {
~It is odd degree with positive leading coefficient. #The highest exponent is 4, which is even.
~It has no x-intercepts. #x<sup>4</sup> - 16 = 0 has real solutions.
=It is even degree with positive leading coefficient. #Correct. That tells both ends rise before using intercepts for the middle behavior.
~Its leading coefficient is -16. #The leading coefficient is 1.
}

::ALG2_U02_L05_Q24::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L05_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>For f(x) = (x + 2)(x - 1)(x - 3), which x-values should be marked as intercepts? {
~x = 2, -1, and -3 #These reverse all the signs.
~x = -2 only #There are three linear factors.
~x = 0, 1, and 3 #x = 0 is not a zero of this form.
=x = -2, 1, and 3 #Correct. Each linear factor gives an x-intercept by setting it equal to zero.
}

::ALG2_U02_L05_Q25::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L05_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>What should a sketch do at a zero with multiplicity 4? {
=touch and turn, often flatter #Correct. Multiplicity 4 is even, so the graph touches, and higher multiplicity can flatten.
~cross sharply #Crossing is typical of odd multiplicity.
~create a hole #Polynomial graphs are continuous.
~create a vertical asymptote #Polynomials do not have vertical asymptotes.
}

